        "content": "## REMAP: five-step framework for goals-of-care conversations\nEach step scored on a 1-5 global scale. Total 9/25 (all 5 steps assessable).\n\n## Scorecard\n| Step | Score |\n| --- | --- |\n| R · Reframe | 2/5 · Fair |\n| E · Expect emotion & empathize | 3/5 · Good |\n| M · Map the future | 1/5 · Poor |\n| A · Align with values | 1/5 · Poor |\n| P · Plan | 2/5 · Fair |\n| **Total** | **9/25** |\n\n### R · Reframe: 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** \"he is on, uh, a machine that breathes for him, and he's on medications that's supporting his heart at this point... he can't take care of them on his own at this moment.\" And to the direct question about improvement: \"I really don't know what-what is gonna happen, a- and I wish I did.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner conveyed clinical reality plainly and honestly: the father's heart and lungs are failing and being externally supported: which is a partial reframe. But there was no clear headline signaling \"we are in a different place now,\" and no pivot to what this means going forward or an invitation to talk about next steps. The daughter opened by asking what her dad would go through if his heart stops, which is an explicit prompt to reframe toward goals; the learner never oriented to that. Honest but incomplete, so Fair.\n- **Recommendations:** After sharing the clinical picture, add a headline and pivot: \"The fact that his body needs machines to do the work of both his heart and lungs tells me he is very sick: sicker than the pneumonia alone. Given where we are, I think it would help to talk about what matters most to your dad, so the care we give fits him.\"\n\n### E · Expect emotion & empathize: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** \"I hear the worry in your voice, and, and I can recognize that it feels confusing that he came in for one thing and now he looks different.\" And \"like you, I'm also worried about him.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner named and reflected emotion competently and early, and normalized the confusion: genuine NURSE-style naming and self-disclosure. However, the encounter closes on the daughter's most vulnerable statement: \"I feel like I've already failed him\": which the transcript leaves unanswered. The learner also missed the chance to invite elaboration (\"tell me more about what worries you\"). Consistent early empathy but with a significant signal not yet met, so Good rather than Very good.\n- **Recommendations:** When she says she failed him, respond directly to the guilt: \"You have not failed him. Coming here, trying to honor what he told you: that is exactly what a loving daughter does. Tell me more about what he said about machines.\"\n\n### M · Map the future: 1/5 · Poor\n- **Evidence:** No corresponding moment in the encounter. The daughter offered a clear opening: \"he told me once, more than once, that he never wanted to be kept alive by machines\": with no learner response captured.\n- **Rationale:** The learner never explored what matters most to the patient, elicited goals, or asked about fears and hopes, despite the daughter volunteering the single most important value (her father's wish not to be kept alive by machines). The task was essentially not performed.\n- **Recommendations:** Pick up her disclosure and map: \"You mentioned he told you he never wanted to be kept alive by machines. Can you tell me more about that: what kind of life mattered to him, and what would he say if he could see himself now?\"\n\n### A · Align with the patient's values: 1/5 · Poor\n- **Evidence:** No corresponding moment in the encounter.\n- **Rationale:** No values were reflected back or synthesized, and none was confirmed. Because Map was not performed, there was nothing to align to. The step was not performed.\n- **Recommendations:** Once values surface, synthesize and confirm: \"So it sounds like what mattered most to your dad was living independently and not being sustained by machines: do I have that right?\"\n\n### P · Plan: 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** \"So, I always have hope, and I never want you to lose hope. And when I hear about hope, I always have to balance it with realism and also worries.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner set up the frame of balancing hope and realism, which lays groundwork for an eventual recommendation, and honestly declined to over-promise. But no recommendation tied to values was made, and the daughter's actual question: what her dad would go through if his heart stops (CPR/code status): was never addressed with a plan or a recommendation. The learner offloaded the situation to uncertainty without offering guidance. Some scaffolding present, so Fair.\n- **Recommendations:** After mapping values, make an explicit recommendation and check reaction: \"Given what you've told me your dad wanted, I would recommend we focus on keeping him comfortable and not attempt CPR if his heart stops, because it would not get him back to the life he valued. What do you think about that?\"\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths**: The learner opened warmly, oriented to the daughter's existing understanding (\"what it is that you've heard\"), and named emotion early and genuinely, including honest self-disclosure of shared worry. Clinical information was delivered plainly and truthfully without false reassurance.\n- **Priority gaps**: The conversation stalled at reframe and never advanced to the heart of the encounter. The daughter twice handed the learner the central value: her father's explicit wish not to be kept alive by machines: and closed on raw guilt (\"I feel like I've already failed him\"), yet neither was taken up. No goals were mapped, no values aligned, and no recommendation was offered to her direct question about what her dad would face. The tone was compassionate but the task drifted; the daughter was left doing the emotional and decisional work alone.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Respond directly to her guilt before anything else: relieve the \"I failed him\" burden explicitly.\n  2. Map values by seizing her own words about machines and independence, then reflect and confirm them.\n  3. Make a clear, values-based recommendation about resuscitation and next steps, and check her reaction: do not leave the decision on her shoulders.",
